"""Original lesson applications for quantitative methods, econometrics, and strategies."""

APPLICATIONS = [
    ("2.1", "household", "Discounting puts a future tuition bill and today's savings on the same date.", "A fictional tuition bill of Rp12.1 million is due in two years. At an assumed 10% annual return, calculate its present value and explain the danger of treating that return as guaranteed.", "The present value is Rp10 million because 12.1 / 1.1² = 10. This is an equivalence at an assumed rate, not a promise that risky savings will fund a fixed bill. Test a lower return and keep the deadline explicit."),
    ("2.6", "warung", "A refrigerator purchase trades cash today for uncertain future operating savings.", "A shop pays 20 now and expects year-end savings of 12 in each of two years. Calculate NPV at 10%, then ask whether the shop can afford the initial outlay.", "NPV is about 0.83: −20 + 12/1.1 + 12/1.1². Positive NPV under these assumptions does not supply the cash needed today. Check maintenance, resale value, and whether the projected savings actually arrive."),
    ("2.7", "bca_split", "Corporate actions must be normalized before returns are summarized or compared.", "A fictional holding has 100 shares at 50 before a five-for-one split and 500 at 10.2 afterward. Compute the ownership return and contrast it with a raw-price calculation.", "Value rises from 5,000 to 5,100, a 2% return. Comparing 10.2 with 50 would report a false 79.6% loss. Store split factors and effective dates; do not adjust both price and share count twice in the same valuation."),
    ("2.8", "warung", "Averages hide how an unusual day changes a shop's inventory and cash requirements.", "Five fictional daily sales observations are 10, 11, 9, 10, and 60. Find the mean and median, then decide what extra evidence is needed before removing the last observation.", "The mean is 20 and median 10. The large sale could be a holiday order, a duplicate, or an error. Investigate provenance before deleting it; a real demand spike matters for inventory even if it is atypical."),
    ("2.9", "calibration", "An alert's precision depends on how common the event is, even when the detector sounds accurate.", "Out of 1,000 hypothetical orders, 10 are faulty. A monitor catches 9 of those but also flags 99 normal orders. Estimate the probability an alerted order is faulty.", "There are 108 alerts and 9 true faults, so precision is about 8.33%. The low base rate dominates the interpretation. A review queue needs this probability and the cost of missed faults, not only the 90% detection rate."),
    ("2.10", "household", "Diversification depends on co-movement as well as each asset's standalone variability.", "Two hypothetical assets each have 20% volatility. Compute an equal-weight portfolio's volatility with correlation zero and with correlation one, assuming the same measurement horizon.", "With zero correlation, volatility is about 14.14%; with perfect positive correlation it is 20%. The weights alone do not determine protection. Reconsider the correlation assumption under a common shock affecting income and investments together."),
    ("2.2", "flash_crash", "A convenient distribution can miss the frequency and severity of stressed market moves.", "A model assumes normal one-day returns with 1% volatility. Design a comparison against a sample containing a −10% day: inspect a histogram, tail quantiles, and exceedance counts.", "A ten-standard-deviation move is extraordinarily unlikely under that fitted normal assumption. Check data integrity first, then consider heavier tails, changing volatility, and dependence. One observation neither proves a universal alternative model nor justifies ignoring the stress."),
    ("2.11", "overload", "Arrival counts become operationally important when requests come in bursts rather than independently.", "A fictional feed averages 100 messages per second but sometimes sends 1,000 together. Compare simulations with independent arrivals and clustered arrivals, keeping the same long-run mean.", "The two simulations can share an average rate while producing very different queue peaks and waiting times. Track maximum backlog, oldest-message age, and rejected work. Capacity planning based only on the mean misses the burst that makes a signal stale."),
    ("2.3", "causal", "A sample estimate needs an uncertainty range before a program's apparent gain is generalized.", "For 100 hypothetical independent shops, mean sales improvement is 4 and sample standard deviation is 10. Form an approximate 95% interval using 1.96 and explain its scope.", "The standard error is 1, giving approximately 2.04 to 5.96. This interval concerns the sampled mean under the assumptions. It does not remove selection bias or establish that training caused the improvement, and clustered shops may require a different standard error."),
    ("2.12", "forecasting", "Consecutive business observations often share conditions, making row-by-row resampling misleading.", "A distributor has two years of daily demand with week-long holiday effects. Outline a bootstrap for mean demand that preserves local dependence, and compare it with independent row resampling.", "Resample contiguous blocks, retaining the order inside each block, and report sensitivity to block length. Independent rows break the holiday clusters and can understate uncertainty. Neither method repairs missing stores or a sample selected only from successful branches."),
    ("2.4", "factors", "Testing a fund's excess return asks whether the estimated effect is distinguishable from sampling noise.", "A hypothetical monthly alpha estimate is 0.3% with standard error 0.2%. Compute its t statistic and state what a failure to reject zero does and does not establish.", "The statistic is 1.5. Under a conventional two-sided large-sample 5% test this would not reject zero, but it does not prove alpha is exactly zero. Check the sample, return units, model specification, and dependence before interpreting the result."),
    ("2.13", "ml_pipeline", "Trying many stock signals creates chances to select a lucky result with no repeatable advantage.", "A team tests 100 null signals at a 5% significance level. Calculate the expected false positives, then propose a research log and a separate evaluation period.", "The expected count is five under valid individual null tests; independence is not needed for that expectation. Log all trials, apply an appropriate multiplicity procedure, and keep a later evaluation untouched. Reporting only the winner hides the selection process."),
    ("2.5", "factors", "Regression turns a fund-market scatterplot into an explicit exposure estimate with a residual.", "In a hypothetical fitted model, fund excess return equals 0.1% plus 1.2 times market excess return. For a 2% market excess return and 3% observed fund return, find prediction and residual.", "The prediction is 2.5% and residual 0.5 percentage points. The slope describes fitted association in the sample. It does not promise the same exposure in a crisis or show that the residual came from skill."),
    ("2.14", "forecasting", "Inventory planning needs uncertainty about the next observation, not only about average demand.", "A shop's predicted weekly demand is 100 units. Explain why an interval for mean demand is insufficient for deciding whether 105 units will cover next week's orders.", "A prediction interval includes uncertainty from a new week's variation as well as estimated parameters, so it is generally wider. Match the horizon and service objective, inspect forecast errors, and state the stockout and overstock costs before choosing inventory."),
    ("2.15", "causal", "A program comparison can change after location and pre-existing business differences enter the model.", "Model fictional shop sales using training participation, prior sales, and district indicators. Specify a reference district and explain how to interpret the participation coefficient.", "Omit one district indicator when including an intercept to avoid an exact linear dependency. The participation coefficient is a conditional association given included variables. Unobserved motivation or selection can still confound it; adding controls alone does not establish causality."),
    ("2.16", "factors", "Highly similar factors can make individual coefficients unstable even when fitted returns barely move.", "A fund regression includes two nearly identical market indexes. Propose diagnostics for coefficient instability and residual dependence, and explain why dropping a factor needs a substantive rationale.", "Inspect correlations, conditioning, coefficient sensitivity, and residual patterns. Robust covariance can address certain inference problems but cannot create independent information in duplicate predictors. Choose the economic exposure definition before interpreting either coefficient as a distinct source of return."),
    ("2.17", "forecasting", "Growing sales and growing market indexes can appear related simply because both trend over time.", "Plot two fictional trending level series and their growth rates. Before regressing one level on the other, specify checks for trend, unit roots, and an economically plausible long-run relation.", "High level correlation can arise without a useful relation. Inspect transformations, breaks, and stationarity diagnostics rather than automatically differencing everything. A supported long-run relation may need a cointegration model; otherwise a levels regression can be misleading."),
    ("2.18", "forecasting", "A forecast that worked in calm months may underestimate errors after volatility increases.", "Compare a rolling model with a last-observation baseline over six held-out months. Record each prediction before its actual value and split the error summary into calm and volatile periods.", "Calculate errors using only forecasts available at each origin. Report the same loss for both models and inspect interval coverage in both periods. A lower aggregate error can conceal a failure exactly when inventory or risk decisions are most sensitive."),
    ("2.19", "ml_pipeline", "A prediction objective must be separated from the future dates used to judge it.", "Design a stock-return pipeline with an imputer, scaler, and regularized regression. Show which dates fit each step and where a regularization parameter can be selected.", "Fit transformations and model parameters within training folds; choose regularization using chronological validation. Reserve a later test period for the frozen procedure. Scaling the complete dataset before splitting gives future distribution information to the training process."),
    ("2.20", "calibration", "A useful classification threshold depends on the economic payoff attached to each outcome.", "A hypothetical trade gains 3 if successful, loses 2 otherwise, and costs 0.2 either way. Find the break-even success probability and compare it with a default 0.5 threshold.", "Expected payoff is 3p − 2(1−p) − 0.2 = 5p − 2.2, so break-even is 0.44. This requires credible probabilities and payoff assumptions. Accuracy alone does not describe expected value, exposure, or implementation feasibility."),
    ("2.21", "edgar", "Financial-period dates and public-availability dates answer different questions in a stock screen.", "A fictional December-year-end filing becomes public on 20 March. A signal is formed on 1 March. Design a join that excludes its figures while preserving the older available filing.", "Join on availability at or before the signal time, retaining measurement period, filing identifier, and revision history. A December period label does not make the number known in December. Later amendments must not overwrite the historical evidence."),
    ("2.22", "llm_evidence", "Text features need a reproducible document history and protection from instructions embedded in the source.", "A fictional filing includes a sentence telling an assistant to report a positive outlook regardless of the figures. Define an extraction record and a test that prevents this text from changing the research rules.", "Store document hash, passage, publication time, extraction version, and validated output. Treat document text as data, constrain the output schema, and check adversarial fixtures. Preserve the rejected passage as evidence without granting it authority over tools or conclusions."),
    ("2.23", "factors", "Factor attribution separates modeled common exposure from the remaining portfolio return.", "A fictional portfolio has market beta 1.1 and size beta 0.3. Market and size factors return 2% and −1%. With zero intercept, calculate modeled return and the residual against 2.4% actual excess return.", "Modeled excess return is 1.9%, leaving 0.5 percentage points unexplained. Keep factor and portfolio units and dates aligned. The residual includes omitted exposures and noise as well as any possible skill; it is not automatically a tradable alpha."),
    ("2.24", "factors", "Active weights can hide a common factor bet across apparently different securities.", "A fictional portfolio's market beta is 1.3 while its benchmark beta is 1.0. Estimate the active-return contribution of a −5% market move, assuming other exposures and residuals are zero.", "Active beta is 0.3, implying a −1.5 percentage-point contribution. This is a conditional scenario, not a full loss forecast. Add other factor exposures, residual risk, and changes in beta before treating it as a portfolio stress estimate."),
    ("2.25", "ldi", "Tail summaries need to be read alongside cash calls that can force liquidation.", "Ten equally likely fictional losses are 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10, and 20. Using nearest-rank 80% VaR and the worst two outcomes for ES, compute both.", "Under the stated convention VaR is 5 and ES is 15. Other finite-sample quantile conventions can differ, so name yours. Neither number by itself specifies when cash must be delivered or how forced selling changes losses."),
    ("2.26", "svb", "A risk scenario must combine price changes with funding timing and sale capacity.", "A fictional balance sheet holds cash 8 and bonds valued at 100. Stress withdrawals are 25 and only 10 of bonds can be sold today. Calculate the immediate gap before considering sale discounts.", "Available cash is at most 18, leaving a gap of 7. Price haircuts could make it worse. A valuation-only stress misses the timing constraint; test funding access, realizable proceeds, and concentrated withdrawal behavior together."),
    ("2.27", "bca_split", "A backtest needs the information and instruments that actually existed at each simulated date.", "Audit a fictional stock screen that uses today's members and unadjusted historical prices. Write two fixtures: a delisted company and a five-for-one split, with the expected behavior of each.", "The delisted company remains eligible on historical dates when it existed, with an explicit exit and missing-data policy. The split preserves economic value before market movement. Correct return arithmetic cannot repair a universe chosen with hindsight."),
    ("2.28", "reporting", "A research report becomes useful when another analyst can reproduce the decision from its evidence.", "Prepare a one-page handoff for an Indonesian-stock factor study: question, dated data, comparison model, validation window, uncertainty, implementation costs, and one reason to withhold a favorable conclusion.", "Link each reported result to an input snapshot and code version. Separate estimated association, out-of-sample performance, and executable economics. State where local data or factor definitions limit transfer from the cited international workflow, and disclose unsuccessful trials."),

    ("3.6", "causal", "A precise estimand distinguishes a policy effect from an association among participating businesses.", "A district wants to know whether offering bookkeeping training raises annual shop sales. Define population, treatment, outcome, horizon, and whether you seek the effect of the offer or attendance.", "An offer effect includes nonattendance and differs from the effect among actual attendees. Specify the comparison population before selecting a regression. Shops choosing to attend may already differ in motivation or resources, making a simple attendee comparison inadequate."),
    ("3.7", "factors", "Return regressions can fail before fitting because observations use incompatible dates or units.", "A fictional fund file reports monthly returns as decimals while a factor file uses percent. Design an alignment audit for dates, missing months, risk-free returns, and unit conversion.", "Convert once to a common convention, join on the same month, and subtract the matching risk-free return only where required. Keep an audit of discarded rows. A 100-fold scaling error can produce plausible-looking coefficient output with the wrong economic meaning."),
    ("3.8", "warung", "Least squares makes the line through advertising and shop sales explicit and inspectable.", "Use invented observations x = [1, 2, 3] and sales y = [2, 3, 5]. Calculate the OLS slope and intercept with an intercept included.", "The slope is 1.5 and intercept 1/3. Residuals sum to zero up to rounding. This describes a small constructed sample; seasonality, reverse causation, and customer demand prevent interpreting the slope as the causal return on advertising."),
    ("3.1", "factors", "A fund's fitted alpha depends on the exposures included and the validity of its residual assumptions.", "Fit a hypothetical fund to market, size, and value factors. Prepare diagnostic plots for residual variance, time dependence, and influential months before presenting an alpha estimate.", "Report coefficient uncertainty beside economic magnitude, inspect whether a few months drive the result, and test a justified specification change. A visually good fit is insufficient if inference assumes independent constant-variance errors that the diagnostics contradict."),
    ("3.9", "factors", "A joint exposure question cannot be answered by scanning separate coefficient p-values.", "A reviewer asks whether size and value jointly contribute to a fictional fund model. State the two restrictions, the unrestricted and restricted models, and an appropriate joint test.", "The null sets both coefficients to zero. Compare against a model retaining both, using an F or Wald procedure whose covariance assumptions fit the data. Correlated estimates mean two separate tests do not reproduce the joint hypothesis."),
    ("3.10", "causal", "Large and small shops may have different error variability even under the same sales model.", "In a fictional training evaluation, residual spread increases with baseline revenue. Compare heteroskedasticity-robust inference with weighted least squares, and identify what must justify the weights.", "Robust covariance adjusts uncertainty without changing OLS coefficients. WLS changes the fit and needs a defensible variance model; arbitrary revenue weights can target a different quantity. Neither approach eliminates omitted variables or selection into training."),
    ("3.11", "factors", "Overlapping returns can make a monthly sequence contain less independent information than its row count suggests.", "A fund study uses rolling three-month returns sampled monthly. Explain why neighboring residuals may correlate and specify a HAC sensitivity check for the alpha interval.", "Adjacent observations share underlying months. Use a stated HAC lag convention and compare plausible bandwidths, reporting sample size and overlap. HAC changes estimated uncertainty; it does not cure a biased return definition or guarantee reliable inference in a very short sample."),
    ("3.12", "causal", "The reason a business joins a program can also explain its later performance.", "Motivated shop owners both attend training and extend opening hours. Draw a causal sketch and explain what goes wrong if motivation is omitted from a sales regression.", "Participation can pick up the effect of motivation, so its coefficient need not equal the training effect. Seek a credible design or measured confounders with justified timing. Adding a post-training variable can also block part of the effect being estimated."),
    ("3.13", "forecasting", "A persistent business series requires separating ordinary fluctuations from trends and structural change.", "A distributor expands from one warehouse to three halfway through a monthly demand series. Plan plots and stationarity checks that distinguish the expansion from gradual stochastic persistence.", "Mark the known expansion, inspect levels and changes, and consider a level-shift or capacity variable. Unit-root test results depend on trend and break specification. Do not difference away business structure solely to obtain a preferred p-value."),
    ("3.14", "forecasting", "An autoregressive forecast translates persistence into a conditional next-period estimate.", "A fictional centered demand process follows x(t) = 0.8x(t−1) + error. Today's deviation is 10. Calculate one- and two-step forecasts, assuming future errors have mean zero.", "The forecasts are 8 and 6.4 above the long-run baseline. Uncertainty expands with horizon even as the point forecast decays. Check whether seasonality, new capacity, or a regime change makes this simple persistence model inadequate."),
    ("3.15", "forecasting", "Choosing a forecasting model should resemble the repeated decisions the business will actually make.", "A distributor orders monthly. Design expanding-window evaluation for an AR model and a seasonal-naive baseline, both forecasting one month ahead on identical dates.", "At each origin, fit with observations available then, save the forecast, and score after the outcome arrives. Compare a preselected loss and error distribution. Choosing the model on the final evaluation period turns that period into another validation set."),
    ("3.16", "bi_rate", "Rates, exchange rates, and returns can improve each other's forecasts without establishing a policy effect.", "For fictional monthly rate changes and currency returns, specify a two-variable VAR and test whether lagged rates improve currency prediction beyond its own lags.", "The restriction concerns predictive information in the chosen system and lag order. Rejection is often called Granger causality, but does not isolate an exogenous monetary-policy shock. Common news, omitted variables, and release timing can explain the predictive relationship."),
    ("3.2", "forecasting", "Two related product prices may share a long-run level while their short-run spread fluctuates.", "Construct fictional wholesale and retail price series with a shared trend and a stationary markup error. Contrast a levels correlation with a test of the estimated long-run residual.", "High correlation alone does not establish cointegration. Specify deterministic terms, estimate the relation, and use the appropriate residual-based critical values. A stable historical relation remains a hypothesis that can break when contracts, taxes, or distribution costs change."),
    ("3.17", "forecasting", "Error correction describes how a temporary departure from a business relation is reduced over time.", "A fictional retail price is 5 above its estimated equilibrium, and the error-correction coefficient is −0.3. Calculate the next-period adjustment contribution with other terms held at zero.", "The error-correction term contributes −1.5, reducing the deviation to 3.5 under the simplification. Check the sign convention and whether both prices adjust. This conditional calculation does not guarantee a profitable pairs trade after costs or a stable future relationship."),
    ("3.18", "ldi", "Volatility persistence changes how long a risk shock influences future position limits.", "A fictional GARCH model has alpha 0.10 and beta 0.85. Compute their sum and explain why a single quiet day should not immediately reset a recently stressed risk limit.", "The sum is 0.95, indicating substantial persistence in this specification. Conditional variance can stay elevated as past shocks decay. Check stationarity assumptions, estimation uncertainty, and liquidity needs; variance dynamics alone do not model collateral calls."),
    ("3.19", "flash_crash", "Large downside moves can reveal weaknesses in symmetric volatility and thin-tail assumptions.", "Compare two fictional variance models after a negative return shock: a symmetric GARCH and a model with an asymmetric shock term. Preselect an out-of-sample variance loss and interval-coverage check.", "Save both forecasts before evaluating subsequent observations and compare the same targets and dates. Asymmetry can help without guaranteeing tail accuracy. Noisy realized-variance proxies and a small number of stress events limit the strength of a model ranking."),
    ("3.20", "factors", "A benchmark model defines what is counted as ordinary exposure before a return is called unexplained.", "For a fictional Indonesian fund, compare a local market-only model with a justified local multifactor model. Explain why substituting U.S. factors without examining the universe may mislead.", "Match investable market, currency, frequency, and economic definitions. A changed intercept can reflect added common exposures rather than changed manager skill. The cited U.S. construction is a methodological reference; transfer to another market needs explicit data and design choices."),
    ("3.21", "factors", "A constant beta can conceal a fund that changed its portfolio policy during the sample.", "A fictional fund shifts from defensive holdings to high-beta stocks halfway through the sample. Plan rolling exposure estimates and a dated policy-break comparison before interpreting full-sample alpha.", "Inspect exposure and residual changes around the recorded shift, with uncertainty for short windows. Full-sample coefficients average different regimes and can misattribute timing effects. Keep the break rationale independent of whichever split produces the most attractive alpha."),
    ("3.3", "household", "Optimization can produce concentrated savings allocations when estimated return differences are mostly noise.", "Two fictional assets have nearly identical estimated risk, but one has a slightly higher sample mean. Compare unconstrained mean-variance weights with an equal-weight baseline and a concentration cap.", "Small input changes can cause large optimized-weight changes. Report out-of-sample behavior, turnover, and sensitivity rather than choosing the most impressive in-sample frontier. A household's bill deadlines and loss capacity impose constraints beyond a statistical objective."),
    ("3.22", "factors", "A covariance estimate can be too noisy to support the precision implied by portfolio weights.", "A desk estimates covariance for 80 stocks from 60 monthly observations. Explain the rank problem and compare shrinkage with a lower-dimensional factor or PCA representation.", "A centered sample covariance has rank at most 59 here and cannot support an ordinary full-rank inverse. Shrinkage or a structured model can stabilize estimation but introduces assumptions. Choose strength or dimension through an appropriate training/validation process, not final-period returns."),
    ("3.23", "causal", "Repeated observations of the same shop require accounting for persistent differences and correlated errors.", "A fictional district tracks 100 shops for 12 months around a training offer. Specify shop and month fixed effects, then choose an inference level consistent with how the offer was assigned.", "Shop effects absorb time-invariant shop differences and month effects absorb common monthly shocks. Cluster uncertainty at an appropriate assignment/dependence level, such as district for district-level offers. Fixed effects do not remove time-varying confounding or solve a lack of comparison variation."),
    ("3.24", "causal", "An instrument needs a credible route into participation and no separate route into the outcome.", "A fictional training lottery offers invitations to some shops. Assess invitation as an instrument for attendance: discuss relevance, exclusion, random assignment, and whose effect would be identified.", "Check whether invitations change attendance and whether an invitation itself provides useful advice or other benefits. Under additional assumptions, IV can identify an effect for compliers rather than every shop. A strong first stage cannot by itself establish exclusion."),
    ("3.25", "causal", "A before-and-after improvement needs a comparison for what would have happened without the program.", "Treated shops' fictional sales rise from 100 to 120 while comparison shops rise from 80 to 90. Calculate difference-in-differences and name the identifying trend assumption.", "The estimate is (120−100) − (90−80) = 10. Interpretation requires credible parallel counterfactual trends and attention to anticipation, spillovers, and composition. Pre-period plots can challenge that assumption but cannot prove the unobserved counterfactual."),
    ("3.26", "calibration", "A binary-outcome model needs probability evaluation before it can support a risk decision.", "A fictional model flags payment delays. Among 100 invoices each predicted at 20% delay probability, 35 are late. Describe the calibration discrepancy and a held-out evaluation design.", "Observed frequency is 35%, suggesting underprediction in that group, with sampling uncertainty. Evaluate reliability and proper probability scores on later invoices. Fit any calibration adjustment on separate training/validation data and account for changed customer mix before selecting a decision threshold."),
    ("3.4", "ldi", "A downside metric should be paired with the cash mechanics that determine whether a portfolio survives.", "A fictional leveraged fund loses 8% in a stress scenario and faces a collateral call due today. Specify a dashboard that includes both return loss and liquidity coverage.", "Show loss, cash available by the deadline, collateral demand, liquidation assumptions, and remaining headroom. A return percentile does not express a same-day funding gap. Test simultaneous price and liquidity changes rather than assuming normal sale capacity during stress."),
    ("3.27", "factors", "A fund's alpha interval should preserve the joint time structure of returns and factors.", "Design a block bootstrap for a hypothetical monthly factor regression. Identify which columns must be resampled together and how block length could alter the estimated alpha uncertainty.", "Resample contiguous rows containing fund return and all factors together, then refit each replication. This preserves local dependence and contemporaneous alignment. Compare reasonable block lengths and disclose limited effective sample size; resampling cannot create missing crisis regimes."),
    ("3.28", "ml_pipeline", "Selecting the best specification changes the evidence required to support its reported significance.", "A team tried 40 feature sets and shows only the best t statistic. Draft the missing trial ledger fields and a validation rule before accepting a new specification.", "Record every feature set, data version, split, metric, timestamp, and result, including failures. Treat selection as part of the procedure and reserve independent evaluation. A conventional p-value for a prespecified model does not automatically apply to the chosen winner."),
    ("3.5", "alfred", "A clean-looking macro backtest can contain revisions that were unavailable when decisions were made.", "Audit a fictional signal that uses the latest downloaded unemployment history for a 2018 decision. Specify the vintage lookup and what to do when historical availability cannot be verified.", "Use the release vintage available at the decision time and preserve the retrieval evidence. If vintage data is missing, label the test's limitation or restrict the sample. A fixed lag applied to the latest revised values does not necessarily remove revision leakage."),
    ("3.29", "factors", "A replication should make the path from published factor definitions to alpha inference inspectable.", "Create a handoff checklist for a fictional fund regression with HAC: dated files, units, excess-return construction, intercept, factor definitions, lag choice, coefficients, and sensitivity output.", "Another analyst should reproduce the row count and estimates from the same files without guessing transformations. Compare conventional and HAC uncertainty with the same coefficients. Explain discrepancies before interpreting significance, and keep the fund's results explicitly separate from the factor documentation."),
    ("3.30", "forecasting", "A synthetic long-run relation provides a known target for testing a cointegration workflow.", "Generate a seeded common random walk and two price series with a stationary difference, then introduce a later markup break. State what your diagnostics should find before and after the break.", "The pre-break design has a long-run relation by construction; the break challenges a constant-parameter model. Keep generation truth separate from fitted estimates, test sensitivity, and report false conclusions. Success on synthetic data establishes a useful fixture, not evidence of a real tradable pair."),
    ("3.31", "svb", "An allocation review must connect estimated risk to a plausible funding stress and operational decision.", "Build a fictional portfolio review with a covariance model, rate shock, cash-withdrawal scenario, and maximum sale capacity. Specify which failure would force an allocation revision even if expected return is attractive.", "A breached cash deadline or exposure limit is an actionable failure independent of mean return. Show the balance sheet before and after each scenario, include uncertainty in realizable prices, and explain which assumptions are supported by data versus deliberately stressed."),
    ("3.32", "reporting", "An econometric dossier has to distinguish a fitted result from a defensible investment conclusion.", "Write the Cedar dossier's final decision using one factor result, one robustness check, one failed diagnostic, and a specific next research step. Attach the files that reproduce all four.", "The conclusion should be conditional on evidence and clearly state whether the study supports further research, paper evaluation, or rejection. Disclose model selection, uncertainty, data limits, and implementation costs. A polished coefficient table cannot replace a complete evidence trail."),

    ("4.5", "warung", "An investment intuition becomes testable when it identifies measurable evidence and a failure condition.", "Translate 'cash-generating businesses should outperform' into a fictional stock-screen hypothesis with a dated cash-flow measure, holding horizon, benchmark, and condition that would count against it.", "Define the universe and measurement before seeing results, including treatment of negative cash flow and missing filings. Specify economic performance after costs, not only a favorable average rank. A test can reject the proposed rule without disproving every form of business analysis."),
    ("4.6", "factors", "A strategy's apparent advantage may come from ordinary exposure already available in a simple comparator.", "A fictional small-company strategy beats a broad index. Propose a size-aware comparator, an equal-weight eligible-universe baseline, and a null signal using the same execution assumptions.", "Align dates, universes, costs, and rebalance rules across comparisons. If the advantage disappears against matched exposure, describe that result rather than claiming unique stock selection. The factor reference helps define the question; it does not supply local executable returns automatically."),
    ("4.7", "knight", "A signal is only one event in a sequence that can create real position commitments.", "Write a hypothetical event trace from a 16:00 close signal to next-session submission, acknowledgement, partial fill, and cancellation. Mark the moment each record can change cash or reserved exposure.", "A signal does not fill at the already observed close. Reserve commitments when submitting according to the broker contract, update executed positions only from fills, and retain cancellation races. Replay the sequence to check that every economic change has an event source."),
    ("4.8", "model_release", "A reproducible strategy includes data and environment identity as well as the chosen parameters.", "Draft a manifest for a fictional value strategy: input hashes, code version, universe rules, parameters, random seed, dependency versions, and output locations. Identify which edits require a new experiment ID.", "Any change that can affect results should produce a distinguishable experiment, including corrected data and execution assumptions. Keep prior artifacts addressable. Reusing a friendly filename for a changed result prevents a reviewer from reconstructing why the strategy was selected."),
    ("4.9", "edgar", "A fundamental screen must wait until a filing was available and preserve later amendments separately.", "A fictional issuer files annual earnings on 15 March and corrects them on 2 May. Specify the value eligible for signals on 1 March, 1 April, and 1 June.", "Use the last older public filing on 1 March, the March filing on 1 April, and the correction on 1 June if available by the decision time. Keep the original values for historical replay. A fiscal-year label cannot stand in for publication time."),
    ("4.10", "bca_split", "Historical membership and corporate actions define what a simulated investor could actually own.", "Create a fictional fixture containing a split, a later delisting, and a missing quote. Describe expected universe membership, adjusted position value, and the rule for an unexecutable rebalance.", "Preserve historical members through their valid dates and apply split transformations consistently. Missing quotes require an explicit policy, not a free exit at the last price. Include delisting proceeds or a disclosed conservative assumption instead of silently dropping the losing stock."),
    ("4.11", "warung", "A ratio can become an extreme rank because its denominator is tiny rather than because the business is exceptional.", "Three fictional companies have operating profit of 10 and revenue of 100, 50, and 0.01. Inspect the margins and propose a data-quality and ranking policy before selecting the highest.", "Margins are 10%, 20%, and 100,000%. Investigate the last record's units and reporting basis before treating it as opportunity. Define denominator validity, missingness, and robust transformation rules using training information, preserving an audit of excluded records."),
    ("4.12", "ml_pipeline", "Labels extending into the next month can leak evaluation outcomes across a date split.", "A fictional sample dated 25 January has a 20-trading-day forward-return label. Validation begins 1 February. Explain how to assign or purge this observation at the boundary.", "Its outcome interval overlaps validation, so a training set ending in January is not automatically safe. Use actual label-end timestamps to remove overlapping training intervals. Keep related same-date stock rows together and document any embargo justified by the information process."),
    ("4.1", "warung", "A liquidation-value screen depends on whether assets can become cash after all senior claims.", "A fictional company has cash 20, receivables 30, inventory 40, and total liabilities 55. Calculate NCAV, then repeat with 80% receivable recovery and 50% inventory recovery.", "Unstressed NCAV is 35; stressed residual is 9. A low market value relative to 35 can look less protective under realizable values. The screen still needs governance, cash-burn, and catalyst analysis before any portfolio decision."),
    ("4.2", "warung", "Business quality and purchase price answer different parts of the investment decision.", "A fictional shop earns sustainable owner cash of 10 annually with no growth. Compare prices of 80 and 200 using a simple cash yield, then identify evidence needed for any growth assumption.", "The yields are 12.5% and 5%. Strong operations do not make every price equally attractive. Any growth case must include reinvestment needs and competitive durability; multiplying recent growth indefinitely can erase the discipline of the valuation comparison."),
    ("4.13", "ge_disclosure", "A cheap valuation can persist when the evidence supporting a recovery is weak or changes.", "For a fictional troubled issuer, separate a signed asset-sale agreement, a management aspiration, and a rumor. Define one thesis-invalidating event and a review date for a value strategy.", "Assign different evidential weight and completion risk to each item. A documented catalyst still may fail or arrive too late relative to cash burn. State what would change the decision before a price decline encourages a new justification for holding."),
    ("4.14", "edgar", "Turning a fundamental screen into holdings requires an auditable bridge from filings to portfolio weights.", "Design a fictional five-stock research packet showing each source filing, screen calculation, exclusion reason, liquidity check, proposed weight, and next review trigger.", "A reviewer should trace every selected and rejected company to information available at the ranking date. Apply portfolio and liquidity constraints after the screen, with a clear handling of insufficient eligible names. A ranked spreadsheet alone is not an executable portfolio."),
    ("4.15", "bi_rate", "A trend strategy can react to changing prices without claiming to forecast central-bank decisions.", "Create a fictional currency-price trend rule formed after each close and traded at the next eligible observation. Compare behavior around an invented rate shock with a buy-and-hold baseline.", "Keep the policy release as contextual annotation rather than future knowledge in the signal. State lookback, volatility scaling, execution time, and costs. A successful path after one shock does not establish reliable forecasting of policy or performance across sideways markets."),
    ("4.16", "factors", "Cross-sectional momentum depends on the precise ranking window and the universe available at formation.", "At a fictional month-end, rank stocks using months 12 through 2 before formation, skipping the latest month. Write the date boundaries and a rule for incomplete histories.", "The skip period must be excluded consistently from both feature construction and explanation. Rank only eligible historical members, with predetermined treatment of missing returns. Compare against the same universe and costs so a momentum result is not simply a membership or liquidity artifact."),
    ("4.17", "flash_crash", "Apparent short-horizon reversal may be a change in quoted side rather than a realizable opportunity.", "A fictional midpoint stays 100 while trade prices alternate between bid 99 and ask 101. Calculate the apparent drop from 101 to 99 and explain why buying that decline may not earn it back.", "The printed decline is about 1.98%, although the midpoint is unchanged. A trader buying may pay the ask and later sell at the bid. Test signal construction against quote data, executable prices, fees, and available depth before interpreting reversal as alpha."),
    ("4.18", "overload", "Small changes near a rank threshold can generate repeated orders without meaningful changes in conviction.", "A fictional stock oscillates between ranks 10 and 11 in a top-ten strategy. Compare immediate replacement with an entry-at-8, exit-below-13 buffer over a fixed path.", "The buffer creates hysteresis and can reduce churn, but changes the holdings and may delay useful updates. Compare turnover, costs, and net performance on predeclared parameter neighborhoods. Choose the policy from validation, keeping the final evaluation for the frozen rule."),
    ("4.3", "forecasting", "A pairs hypothesis needs a stable economic relation beyond a visually similar price chart.", "Two fictional distributors share input costs but have different debt and customers. List a plausible common driver, a spread definition, and evidence that would reject a proposed mean-reversion strategy.", "Check dated data, stationarity or cointegration assumptions, and stability across regimes. Debt changes or customer loss can permanently alter relative value. A narrow historical spread alone does not justify leverage or show that a widening spread must revert."),
    ("4.19", "forecasting", "An estimated reversion speed should match the horizon over which a position is expected to pay off.", "A fictional stationary spread follows an AR(1) coefficient of 0.8. Calculate the approximate half-life using log(0.5)/log(0.8), then compare it with a two-day holding limit.", "The half-life is about 3.11 periods, longer than the two-day limit if observations are daily. Estimation uncertainty and changing dynamics matter. A half-life formula is a model implication, not a guarantee of convergence or an estimate of executable profit."),
    ("4.20", "idempotency", "A two-leg strategy can become a directional position while acknowledgements and fills arrive separately.", "A fictional pair order intends long 100 units of A and short 80 of B. A fills completely while B's acknowledgement times out. Define reconciliation and hedge-risk actions without blindly resubmitting B.", "Query or replay B using the original request identity and inspect current fills and commitments. Bound the unhedged exposure and define a timed contingency. A duplicate short or an assumed cancellation can make the imbalance worse; intended neutrality is not actual neutrality."),
    ("4.21", "forecasting", "A relationship break is a strategy failure condition that should be visible in the final research packet.", "Introduce a permanent level shift into a fictional pair after model fitting. Evaluate the frozen entry rule, stop policy, and re-estimation trigger without refitting on the entire path.", "Record losses and exposure during the break, including costs of stopping and restarting. Re-estimation uses only subsequently available information. A capstone should show the failed regime and the control response, rather than presenting only periods where the spread reverted."),
    ("4.4", "ml_pipeline", "A stock-ranking model must generalize across future dates while respecting shared market conditions.", "Design a fictional cross-sectional model with all stocks on a date assigned to the same fold. Compare it with a simple value rank using identical universes, rebalances, and cost assumptions.", "Keep preprocessing and feature selection inside training folds, and aggregate evaluation by date as well as stock. Random stock-row splits can share market regimes across train and test. The baseline comparison should isolate the model's incremental usefulness, not different implementation rules."),
    ("4.22", "ml_pipeline", "Selecting parameters and measuring the selected process require distinct chronological loops.", "Sketch nested walk-forward evaluation for a fictional stock ranker: inner folds choose depth and regularization, while an outer future window evaluates the chosen pipeline.", "Repeat selection using only each outer training history, then freeze the winner before the outer test. Aggregate the sequence of genuinely future predictions. Reusing outer results to choose features or parameters turns the reported evaluation into additional tuning."),
    ("4.23", "calibration", "A strong rank correlation can coexist with trades whose costs consume their expected payoff.", "A fictional model's top bucket has a 0.4% expected holding-period gain over the comparator and estimated round-trip cost of 0.5%. State what ranking metrics and economic checks should accompany the result.", "The simple expected net difference is −0.1 percentage points before other frictions. Report rank stability, bucket spreads, turnover, uncertainty, and cost sensitivity. Better statistical ranking does not alone justify trades when the implementation margin is negative."),
    ("4.24", "ml_pipeline", "Ablations test whether a complex feature contributes information or merely accompanies a favorable backtest.", "A fictional stock model adds a news score. Compare the frozen model with that score removed, shuffled within permitted training data, and delayed to its true publication time.", "Keep splits, random seeds, execution, and other features fixed. A gain that disappears after correcting availability indicates leakage rather than useful news prediction. Negative controls and ablations narrow explanations but still need uncertainty and genuinely future evaluation."),
    ("4.25", "ldi", "Volatility targeting can increase leverage in calm periods and force reductions when liquidity weakens.", "A fictional strategy targets 10% volatility. Its forecast rises from 5% to 20%. Calculate unconstrained exposure multipliers and add a leverage cap and a liquidity-aware adjustment rule.", "The multipliers fall from 2 to 0.5. The fourfold change may demand costly sales during stress. Apply explicit caps, cash requirements, and execution constraints, and evaluate delayed adjustment rather than assuming the target can always be reached instantly."),
    ("4.26", "factors", "Several stock signals can lead to the same portfolio-level sector or factor concentration.", "A fictional value and momentum allocation both select banks, giving 55% combined banking exposure against a 25% cap. Describe how the portfolio construction step should resolve the conflict.", "Aggregate exposures across sleeves before checking constraints. Reallocate or reduce positions with a deterministic policy, documenting the impact on signal objectives and cash. Separate covariance estimates from hard concentration constraints; a favorable estimated correlation does not waive the cap."),
    ("4.27", "flash_crash", "Strategy capacity depends on how much volume remains executable at tolerable cost.", "A fictional rebalance requires selling 30,000 shares in a stock trading 100,000 shares daily. With a 10% participation cap, calculate the minimum number of normal-volume sessions, ignoring other constraints.", "The daily capacity is 10,000 shares, so at least three sessions are needed. Stressed volume and depth may reduce capacity further. Model delayed exposure, spread, impact, and opportunity cost; applying one fixed fee to an instantaneous rebalance misses these effects."),
    ("4.28", "atomic_ledger", "Independent accounting checks reveal profitable-looking backtests that create cash or lose positions.", "A fictional account starts with cash 1,000, buys five shares at 100 with a fee of 2, then marks them at 102. Reconcile cash, position value, and equity in two implementations.", "Cash is 498, holdings are 510, and equity is 1,008. The gain of 8 equals the unrealized gain of 10 less the fee. Compare transaction-level states, including partial fills and corporate actions, rather than only matching final summary metrics."),
    ("4.29", "ml_pipeline", "An untouched period evaluates a frozen research process only while its results remain outside selection decisions.", "A fictional team has used 2015–2022 for design and 2023 for validation, reserving 2024 for final evaluation. Write a rule for what happens if the 2024 result prompts a new feature.", "The new feature starts a new research cycle; 2024 is now observed and cannot honestly remain untouched. Record the change and seek later independent evidence or label the result exploratory. A new random seed does not restore the information barrier."),
    ("4.30", "reporting", "Uncertainty around the selected strategy should disclose how many alternatives competed for selection.", "A fictional report highlights the best of 60 parameter combinations. Add a trial-count disclosure, a dependence-aware uncertainty estimate, and a comparison across nearby parameters.", "Show whether the winner is isolated or part of a stable neighborhood, and preserve every trial in the ledger. Account for selection when making inferential claims. A narrow interval conditional on the chosen strategy can understate uncertainty introduced by the search."),
    ("4.31", "knight", "Implementation readiness requires surviving faulty events as well as ordinary market paths.", "Design three fictional release fixtures: a duplicate acknowledgement, a stale quote during a rebalance, and a mismatched code version on one worker. Specify a pass/fail action for each.", "Duplicates must not duplicate economic effects, stale inputs must trigger the declared gate, and inconsistent release identity must block activation. Preserve incident evidence and reconcile account state before resuming. A favorable backtest does not establish these operational properties."),
    ("4.32", "reporting", "A strategy defense must let a skeptical reviewer trace the result and challenge its practical assumptions.", "Prepare a dossier with the hypothesis, all trials, point-in-time data, baseline, frozen evaluation, accounting reconciliation, cost stress, and a conditional go/no-go conclusion for paper use.", "Every headline number should resolve to a dated artifact. Present failed tests and capacity limits alongside favorable evidence, and distinguish exploratory research from a release candidate. State the next observable condition that would overturn the conclusion rather than promising future returns."),
    (
        "2.29",
        "calibration",
        "A forecast is judged over many resolved calls because one call carries almost no information. The same arithmetic decides whether a hypothesis test could have answered the question it was asked.",
        "Before running a test, compute its power against the smallest effect you would act on, and the minimum effect your actual sample can detect at 80% power.",
        "Power is 1 − Φ(z − λ) + Φ(−z − λ) with λ the effect in standard errors, and the sample size for a target power is ((z_{1−α/2} + z_{1−β})σ/δ)². The square is the part that matters: halving the effect you want to detect quadruples the data required. For 60 monthly observations at σ = 2.00, an effect of 0.40 is found only 34% of the time and the minimum detectable effect is 0.72 a month. Report a non-rejection with those two figures beside it, because without them it is not evidence of absence.",
    ),
    (
        "2.30",
        "reporting",
        "The GIPS standards ask for the method behind a performance figure, not only the figure. An annualised Sharpe ratio carries a hidden method: the square-root-of-time rule, which assumes something about the data that is rarely checked.",
        "Measure the first-order autocorrelation of your return series, compute the variance inflation factor, and report the adjusted annualised volatility and Sharpe ratio beside the conventional ones.",
        "The variance of a sum of T returns is Tσ² plus twice every covariance between them, so σ√T is exact only under independence. At ρ = 0.20 over 252 periods the variance inflation is 1.4975 and the volatility factor its square root, 1.2237: a reported Sharpe of 0.635 is really 0.519, an overstatement of 22.4%. Check it against the long-run limit (1+ρ)/(1−ρ) = 1.5; keeping only the lag-one term gives 1.4 and understates the correction. Stale and smoothed marks produce exactly this, so the strategies whose ratios look best are the ones where the assumption fails hardest. Quote the autocorrelation with the ratio or the ratio is incomplete.",
    ),
    (
        "2.31",
        "forecasting",
        "A forecast maintained over many periods reveals what a single period cannot: how much of an observed lead was persistent and how much was a draw that will not repeat.",
        "Compute the cross-sectional correlation between consecutive periods for a set of funds or signals, then predict each one's next period as the mean plus that correlation times its gap to the mean.",
        "The prediction is x̄ + ρ(x − x̄), so the expected gap shrinks by a factor of ρ each period. At ρ = 0.30 a fund leading by 5.0 points is expected to lead by 1.5, and the fund trailing by 5.0 recovers 3.5 with no change in how it is run. Before attributing either move to complacency or to a remedy, subtract what the arithmetic already predicts; establishing the remainder needs a comparison group selected the same way, not a before-and-after on the tail you picked.",
    ),
    (
        "2.32",
        "edgar",
        "Filings arrive late, get amended, or never arrive. Every one of those is a gap in a column, and a pipeline that reads the gap as a zero has made a claim about the company rather than about the data.",
        "For one column in your own dataset, classify the mechanism behind its gaps, then compute the mean and variance under all three treatments: dropping, zero-filling, and mean-filling.",
        "Zero-filling multiplies the mean by the share of rows present, so two blanks in seven understate it by 28.6%. Mean-filling leaves the mean alone and understates the variance by the ratio of degrees of freedom, 33.3% in the same case; the standard deviation is understated by 18.4% and the standard error of the mean by 31.0%, since filling shrinks the spread and inflates the row count at once. Dropping is honest under MCAR provided the dropped count is reported. A fund that stops reporting after a bad quarter is missing not at random and none of the three repairs it, which is why survivorship bias is a missing-data problem rather than a separate one.",
    ),
]
